<chapter>

<title><classname>HttpdContentSink</classname> Reference</title>
 <section id="HttpdContentSink" xreflabel="HttpdContentSink">
  <title>Introduction</title>
  <para>
  The <classname>HttpdContentSink</classname> implements the interface
  of <xref linkend="wrtAbstract">. Data written to the 
  <classname>HttpdContentSink</classname> is buffered up in memory for later
  use. 
  </para>
  <para>
  The most common use is to direct the output of an operation such that the 
  output data is queued for later transmission. In fact, once stored within
  the <classname>HttpdContentSink</classname> the data can be sent multiple
  times. 
  </para>
  <para>
  Another common use of <classname>HttpdContentSink</classname> is to 
  buffer up data so that a correct <literal>Content-length</literal>
  header can be sent as the result of an <acronym>HTTP</acronym> request.
  </para>
  <note>
   <para>
    Only the methods in addition to those that are part of the abstract
    interface are documented here.
   </para>
  </note>
 </section>

 <section><title>Public Methods</title>

  <section><title><function>ContentLength</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       size_t
       <function><classname>HttpdContentSink</classname>::ContentLength</function>
      </funcdef><void>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
   This function returns the number of bytes queued in the 
   <classname>HttpdContentSink</classname> at the current moment.
   </para>

  </section>

  <section><title><function>SendData</function></title>
   <para>
    <funcsynopsis>
     <funcprototype>
      <funcdef>
       int
       <function><classname>HttpdContentSink</classname>::SendData</function>
      </funcdef>
      <paramdef>HttpdWritable *<parameter>p_data</parameter></paramdef>
     </funcprototype>
    </funcsynopsis>
   </para>

   <para>
   This function writes the queued data to the interface specified by
   <parameter>p_data</parameter>.
   </para>

   <para>
    Upon success, <literal>0</literal> is returned; otherwise a system
    dependent error value is returned (see <xref
    linkend="opsErrorCodes">).
   </para>

  </section>

 </section>

</chapter>

